# QR Ordering SaaS - Cursor Rules

## 1. Project Overview

Production-grade SaaS for **restaurant and food-store owners**: they sign up, create one or more stores, define tables, and print or display **QR codes per table**. **Customers** scan a QR (no account required unless you add it), land on that store’s menu for that table, and place orders. Owners track orders per store and per table in near real time.

Built with Next.js (App Router) and Firebase.

Core flows:
- **STORE_OWNER:** authenticate → manage stores, tables, menus (as features land) → generate QR per table
- **CUSTOMER:** scan QR → `store/{storeId}/table/{tableId}` → browse menu → submit order
- **SUPER_ADMIN:** platform operations

**Scale target:** design and implement for **at least 10k active users** (owners + staff + concurrent diners) without architectural rewrites; avoid patterns that break before **50k+**.

---

## 2. Roles & Access Control

### Roles
- SUPER_ADMIN → Platform owner (full access)
- STORE_OWNER → Owns and manages stores
- CUSTOMER → Places orders via QR

---

## 3. Tech Stack

- Frontend: Next.js (App Router, TypeScript)
- Backend: Firebase (BaaS)
- Database: Firestore
- Authentication: Firebase Auth
- Storage: Firebase Storage
- Server Logic: Firebase Cloud Functions
- State Management:
    - TanStack Query (server state, caching, deduping — tune `staleTime` / `gcTime` for menu and store data to cut Firestore reads)
    - Zustand (lightweight client/UI state)
    - Redux (only for complex global orchestration)
- Forms: React Hook Form + Zod
- Styling: Tailwind CSS v4
- UI primitives: **`@/components/ui`** — `Button`, `Input`, `Select`, `Textarea`, `Label` built with **class-variance-authority**, **`cn()`** (`clsx` + `tailwind-merge` in `src/lib/cn.ts`), **`@radix-ui/react-slot`** for `Button asChild` (e.g. wrap `next/link`). Prefer these over ad-hoc styled `<button>` / `<input>` for consistency.
- Icons: **lucide-react** (consistent stroke icons; import named icons only as needed)
- Theme: **next-themes** (`ThemeProvider` in root `layout.tsx`, `attribute="class"` on `<html>`). Light tokens in `src/app/theme/light.css`; dark tokens when `html.dark` in `theme/dark.css`. Global toggle: **`ThemeToggle`** in `src/components/shared/ThemeToggle.tsx` (Sun / Moon from Lucide). Use `dark:` Tailwind variants for component-level dark styles; prefer CSS variables (`--surface-page`, etc.) for theme-aware colors.

---

## 4. Core Features

### 4.1 Authentication
- Email/Password login
- Secure session handling
- Role-based access control

---

### 4.2 Store Management
- Create, update, delete stores
- Manage store dashboard

---

### 4.3 Table & QR System
- Each store can create multiple tables
- Each table has a unique QR code

QR Format:
https://yourapp.com/store/{storeId}/table/{tableId}

---

### 4.4 Order System
- Customer scans QR → lands on store page
- Table is auto-detected
- Customer places order

Order fields:
- storeId
- tableId
- items[]
- customerDetails (optional)
- status → pending | preparing | completed
- createdAt

---

### 4.5 Real-Time Updates
- Use Firestore listeners **only where live UI is required** (e.g. kitchen / owner order board).
- **Scope every listener** to a single store path (`stores/{storeId}/orders/...`) with `where`, `orderBy`, and **`limit()`**; never attach a listener to an unbounded collection.
- **Paginate** order lists; use cursors (`startAfter` / `endBefore`). **Unsubscribe** on route change or unmount to avoid duplicate streams at scale.

---

## 5. Folder Structure (MANDATORY)

Repository layout (Next.js app under `src/`, Firebase indexes at repo root, tests outside `src/` so tooling stays simple):

my-app/
├── firebase/
│ └── firestore.indexes.json   ← versioned composite indexes; update whenever queries change (§14, §5.2)
├── tests/
│ ├── unit/
│ ├── integration/
│ └── e2e/
├── functions/                 ← optional: add Firebase Cloud Functions here (rate limits, order validation)
└── src/
    ├── app/
    │ ├── (local)/             ← route group: **all UI pages** (name not in URL) — keeps pages separate from API tree
    │ │ ├── (home)/            ← `/` — `page.tsx` + `components/`
    │ │ │ ├── components/
    │ │ │ └── page.tsx
    │ │ ├── (auth)/            ← `/login`, `/signup`
    │ │ │ ├── login/
    │ │ │ └── signup/
    │ │ ├── (dashboard)/       ← `/dashboard`, `/dashboard/stores`
    │ │ │ └── dashboard/
    │ │ ├── (admin)/           ← `/admin`, `/admin/activity`
    │ │ │ └── admin/
    │ │ └── store/[storeId]/table/[tableId]/   ← public QR ordering
    │ │     ├── components/
    │ │     ├── page.tsx
    │ │     ├── loading.tsx
    │ │     └── error.tsx
    │ ├── (api)/               ← route group: **HTTP handlers only** (name not in URL); still need real `api` segment for `/api/*`
    │ │ └── api/
    │ │     └── health/
    │ │         └── route.ts   ← thin `GET` → `NextResponse.json(...)`; logic in `src/server/api/`
    │ ├── layout.tsx           ← root layout; `globals.css`, `theme/`, special files stay beside `(local)` / `(api)`
    │ ├── globals.css          ← Tailwind + `@import` theme files + `@theme` bridge only
    │ └── theme/
    │     ├── light.css        ← light design tokens (`:root`)
    │     └── dark.css         ← dark tokens when `next-themes` sets `class="dark"` on `<html>`
    ├── features/              ← each feature: api.ts, hooks.ts, types.ts, components/
    │ ├── auth/
    │ ├── store/
    │ ├── table/
    │ ├── order/
    │ └── admin/
    ├── components/
    │ ├── ui/                  ← `Button` (variants + sizes), `Input`, `Select`, `Textarea`, `Label`; barrel `index.ts`
    │ └── shared/
    │     ├── ThemeProvider.tsx
    │     ├── ThemeToggle.tsx
    │     ├── AuthProvider.tsx
    │     ├── PageLoading.tsx
    │     ├── PageError.tsx
    │     ├── PageNotFound.tsx
    │     └── GoBackButton.tsx
    ├── lib/
    │ ├── config.ts          ← app configuration: `ENV`, routes, roles, `PRODUCT`, SEO, `appConfig` aggregate
    │ ├── cn.ts              ← `cn()` merge helper for UI class names
    │ ├── site.ts            ← root `metadata` helper (imports `@/lib/config`)
    │ ├── env.ts             ← `getPublicEnv()`, `isFirebaseWebConfigured()` — safe on client or server
    │ ├── env.server.ts      ← `getServerEnv()` — server-only secrets; never import from Client Components
    │ ├── firebase.ts        ← `getFirebaseWebConfig()` reads `env.ts` (wire `initializeApp` when deps added)
    │ └── helpers.ts
    ├── services/
    │ ├── qr.service.ts
    │ └── order.service.ts
    ├── server/
    │ └── api/                 ← HTTP handler logic (called from `app/(api)/api/**/route.ts` only)
    │     └── health/
    │         └── get-health-status.ts
    ├── store/                 ← Zustand slices / client stores
    └── types/                 ← optional cross-feature re-exports (`index.ts`)

**Path alias:** `tsconfig.json` maps `@/*` → `./src/*` (e.g. `@/lib/config`, `@/server/api/health/get-health-status`, `@/components/ui`, `@/features/order/api`).

### 5.0a App route groups — `(local)` vs `(api)` (MANDATORY)

- **`src/app/(local)/`** — every **page** and page-specific `layout.tsx` / `loading.tsx` / `error.tsx` for UI routes. Parentheses mean `(local)` does **not** appear in the URL (`(home)/page.tsx` → `/`, `(local)/store/...` → `/store/...`).
- **`src/app/(api)/api/`** — every **Route Handler** (`route.ts`). The inner folder must be named **`api`** so URLs remain **`/api/...`**; `(api)` is only an organizational wrapper (not in the URL). Example: `(api)/api/health/route.ts` → **`GET /api/health`**.
- Keep **`layout.tsx`**, **`globals.css`**, **`theme/`**, **`global-error.tsx`**, root **`error.tsx`**, **`loading.tsx`**, **`not-found.tsx`** under **`src/app/`** (siblings of `(local)` and `(api)`).

### 5.0 UI components (MANDATORY for forms & actions)

- Import from **`@/components/ui`** (or per-file `@/components/ui/button`, etc.).
- **`Button`** variants: `default`, `secondary`, `outline`, `ghost`, `destructive`, `link`. Sizes: `sm`, `md`, `lg`, `icon`, `icon-sm`, `icon-lg`. Use **`asChild`** to apply styles to `Link` or other elements via Radix `Slot`.
- **`Input`**, **`Select`**, **`Textarea`**: variants `default` | `error` (e.g. validation). Pair with **`Label`** (`htmlFor` / `id`).
- Compose with theme tokens (`border-border`, `bg-background`, `text-muted`); do not hardcode zinc hex for new screens unless matching legacy.

### 5.1 App Router pages — `<Suspense>` (MANDATORY)

- When creating or editing any `page.tsx` under `src/app/`, wrap the page body (the JSX returned by the page) in React `<Suspense>` with a `fallback` (for example a loading skeleton or spinner).
- Import `Suspense` from `react`. Use nested `<Suspense>` when only part of the page should stream or suspend independently.

### 5.1a Route colocation — `components/` next to `page.tsx` (MANDATORY)

- For every route that has a **`page.tsx`**, colocate route-specific UI in a **`components/`** folder **in the same directory as that `page.tsx`** (same pattern as `store/[storeId]/table/[tableId]/components/` + `page.tsx`). **Do not** use a global `src/app/components/` folder — each `page.tsx` has its own sibling `components/`.
- **Root `/`:** Next.js only allows one page at `/`. Put the home **`page.tsx`** under **`src/app/(local)/(home)/page.tsx`** with `components/` beside it (both `(local)` and `(home)` are omitted from the URL).
- **`page.tsx` stays thin:** import a **`*Wrapper`** component from `./components/…` inside `<Suspense>`; the wrapper composes **`*View`** (and hooks) as needed.
- **Naming (component files = PascalCase):** use `SomethingWrapper.tsx` and `SomethingView.tsx` (or feature-prefixed equivalents, e.g. `LoginView.tsx`, `DashboardHomeWrapper.tsx`). Put optional route-only helpers under **`components/shared/`** (same PascalCase for component files).
- **No separate mobile files:** do **not** add `*Mobile.tsx` (or parallel mobile-only trees). Use **one responsive** `*View` with Tailwind breakpoints (`sm:`, `md:`, etc.) or CSS. Do not split Desktop vs Mobile into sibling files.
- **Global vs route components:** `src/components/` (under `src/`, not inside `app/`) remains for **shared** UI (`ui/`, `shared/` like `PageLoading`). **Route-specific** pieces live under `src/app/.../components/`.

### 5.2 Scalability & capacity (MANDATORY — 10k+ users)

- **Firestore reads/writes:** Prefer **narrow queries** + **`limit()`** + **pagination** on orders, tables, and menus. Index every composite query in **`firebase/firestore.indexes.json`** before shipping features that rely on it.
- **Listeners:** One bounded subscription per surface (e.g. one store’s “active orders” view). No global `onSnapshot` on `orders`. Tear down listeners when the view closes.
- **Hot documents:** Avoid a single doc that every write bumps (e.g. global order counter on `stores/{id}`). Use subcollections, sharded counters, or Cloud Functions–mediated aggregates if you need totals.
- **Writes:** Use **batched writes** / **transactions** when updating multiple docs for one business action; avoid N sequential writes when one batch suffices.
- **Public QR pages:** Minimize work on first paint — lazy-load non-critical UI, cache stable data with TanStack Query (`staleTime` for menus), use Next.js **static / ISR** where content is safe to cache; keep **dynamic** only where auth or per-request data demands it.
- **Cloud Functions:** Validate **create order** server-side (store/table exists, payload shape, optional rate limits). Keep handlers **idempotent** where retries are possible.
- **Media:** Menus and photos in **Firebase Storage** with reasonable image sizes; do not embed large binaries in Firestore fields.
- **Bundle:** Code-split owner dashboard vs customer ordering routes; avoid loading admin-only code on public table pages.

---

## 6. Data Modeling Strategy (CRITICAL)

Use **subcollections** so each store shards its own data (scales with number of stores, not one giant `orders` bucket):

stores/{storeId}
  ├── tables/{tableId}
  ├── orders/{orderId}
  └── (optional) menuItems/{itemId} — keep order line items denormalized only as much as needed for display/history

users/{userId}

- Keep **order documents small** (line items as compact structures); archive or TTL old orders if history grows very large (optional product decision, but plan queries accordingly).

---

## 7. Firestore Schema

### users
- id
- email
- role → SUPER_ADMIN | STORE_OWNER | CUSTOMER
- first_name (optional)
- last_name (optional)
- avatar (optional)

---

### stores
- id
- ownerId
- name
- createdAt

---

### tables (subcollection)
- id
- tableNumber
- qrUrl
- createdAt

---

### orders (subcollection)
- id
- tableId
- items[]
- status
- createdAt

---

## 8. Firestore Security Rules (CRITICAL)

- SUPER_ADMIN → full access

- STORE_OWNER:
  - access only their stores (ownerId == auth.uid)

- CUSTOMER:
  - can create orders only

Rules:

- stores:
  - read/write → owner OR SUPER_ADMIN

- tables:
  - read → public (QR)
  - write → owner OR SUPER_ADMIN

- orders:
  - create → public
  - read/update → owner OR SUPER_ADMIN

- NEVER allow unrestricted writes

---

## 9. API & Architecture Rules

### HTTP APIs (Next.js `RouteHandler`) — pages vs API

- **`src/app/(api)/api/**`** holds **only** route wiring: each folder has a thin **`route.ts`** that exports `GET` / `POST` / etc. and returns `NextResponse`. **No business logic** here beyond delegating to `src/server/api/`.
- **`src/server/api/<resource>/`** holds **handlers, response types, and validation** (e.g. Zod). One file per action is fine (`get-health-status.ts`, `create-order.ts`). Import those from `app/(api)/api/.../route.ts`.
- **`src/app/(local)/`** holds all **`page.tsx`** trees so **pages** and **API route files** are physically separated in the repo.

### Client / feature data layer (Firebase, TanStack Query)

- NEVER call Firebase directly in UI
- Use:

features/*/api.ts  
features/*/hooks.ts  
services/*  

Each feature must contain:
- api.ts
- hooks.ts
- types.ts
- components/

---

## 10. Routing & Middleware

### Routes
- /admin/* → SUPER_ADMIN only
- /dashboard/* → STORE_OWNER only
- /store/* → public

### Validation
- storeId must exist
- tableId must belong to store

---

## 11. QR Code Rules

- Generate QR in qr.service.ts
- Always map to:

/store/{storeId}/table/{tableId}

---

## 12. Environment Modes

### Development
- Firebase Emulator
- Debug logs enabled

### Test
- Separate Firebase project
- Mock/test data

### Production
- Live Firebase
- Strict rules
- Logging enabled

---

## 13. Performance Rules

Follow **§5.2** for capacity. Additionally:

- **Firestore:** `limit()` + `orderBy()` on every list query; cursor-based pagination for order history; composite indexes for any multi-field filter + sort.
- **React:** Avoid unnecessary re-renders (stable query keys, memoize expensive child lists when profiling shows benefit); lazy-load heavy dashboard widgets.
- **Next.js:** Prefer static/ISR for marketing and stable public content; use `loading.tsx` / Suspense fallbacks so navigations stay responsive under load.

---

## 14. Firestore Indexing Rules

Define composites for real queries under `stores/{storeId}/orders` (and tables), for example:

- `orders`: `createdAt` DESC (default feed)
- `orders`: `status` ASC + `createdAt` DESC (kitchen filters)
- Any `where` + `orderBy` combination used in production **must** have a matching index; ship **`firebase/firestore.indexes.json`** with the repo.

Use **collection-group queries** only if absolutely required — they need explicit indexes and can be expensive; prefer store-scoped paths.

---

## 15. Error Handling

- Show user-friendly errors
- Handle network failures
- Log all critical errors

---

## 16. Logging & Monitoring

- Log:
  - order creation
  - updates
  - failures

- Tools:
  - Firebase Logs
  - Optional: Sentry

- Never log sensitive data

---

## 17. Testing Rules (STRICT)

- Colocate by kind under repo root: `tests/unit/`, `tests/integration/`, `tests/e2e/` (not inside `src/`).
- Unit tests → business logic (`lib/`, `services/`, pure feature helpers)
- Integration tests → Firebase flows (Emulator)
- E2E tests → QR → order flow

Tools:
- Firebase Emulator Suite
- Jest
- React Testing Library

---

## 18. Abuse Protection

- Rate limit **order creation** (per IP / per table / per store — choose policy in Functions) to protect Firestore and owner UX at volume.
- Validate inputs with **Zod** on the client and **again** in Cloud Functions for anything security- or billing-related.
- Reject obviously invalid `storeId` / `tableId` pairs server-side; consider CAPTCHA or throttles if spam appears.
- Use Firebase Functions for enforcement so rules stay centralized as traffic grows.

---

## 19. Caching Strategy

- TanStack Query: set sensible **`staleTime`** for menus and store metadata; use **`placeholderData`** / prefetch on hover for owner dashboard where it helps.
- Deduplicate identical in-flight requests; avoid refetch-on-window-focus for data that rarely changes unless the UX requires freshness.
- Align caching with **§5.2** — fewer redundant Firestore reads is the main cost and latency lever at 10k+ users.

---

## 20. Deployment Strategy

- Frontend → Vercel
- Backend → Firebase

Environments:
- dev → emulator
- test → staging
- prod → live

Use env variables:
- NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY
- NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID

---

## 21. Naming Conventions

- Components → PascalCase
- Hooks → useSomething
- Files → kebab-case
- Constants → UPPER_SNAKE_CASE

---

## 22. Code Rules (STRICT)

- TypeScript strict mode
- No `any`
- Clean architecture
- Modular design

---

## 23. Important Rules

❌ No business logic in UI  
❌ No direct Firebase calls in components  
❌ No hardcoded values  
❌ No unbounded Firestore queries or listeners  
❌ No `*Mobile.tsx` (or Desktop/Mobile file pairs) under `src/app/` — one responsive `*View` + **§5.1a**  

✅ Modular, testable features  
✅ Route colocation: **`components/`** beside each **`page.tsx`** (**§5.1a**)  
✅ **§5.2** applied to new screens and APIs  
✅ Baseline target: **10k+ active users**; architecture must not block growth toward **50k+** without a deliberate redesign  

